WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.100
Chunking strategies.

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.258
Before you embed a document, you have to cut it up.

3
00:00:05.258 --> 00:00:07.500
Where you cut decides what you can find.

4
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:13.000
Embed a forty page report as one vector and you get semantic mush.

5
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.821
The LLM needs focused context anyway.

6
00:00:15.821 --> 00:00:17.933
So we split the document first.

7
00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:20.867
There is a ladder of strategies.

8
00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:23.771
Fixed size with overlap is the baseline.

9
00:00:23.771 --> 00:00:26.767
Overlap keeps boundary sentences in one piece.

10
00:00:27.067 --> 00:00:30.200
Recursive splitting follows the document's own seams.

11
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:34.500
Semantic splitting cuts where similarity between sentences drops.

12
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.267
And parent-child is the production favorite.

13
00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:40.233
Chunking has one core tension.

14
00:00:40.533 --> 00:00:42.833
Small chunks hold one idea per vector.

15
00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:44.835
So they retrieve precisely.

16
00:00:44.835 --> 00:00:46.600
Big chunks retrieve mushily.

17
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:50.867
But small chunks starve the model of context, while big ones supply it.

18
00:00:51.167 --> 00:00:54.500
Parent-child resolves it: match the small, feed the big.

19
00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:56.800
Here is the flow.

20
00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:00.567
The query is embedded and compared against small child chunks.

21
00:01:00.867 --> 00:01:02.533
The best child wins the search.

22
00:01:02.833 --> 00:01:04.400
We look up its larger parent.

23
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:06.833
And hand that parent to the LLM.

24
00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:10.367
Precision at search time, context at generation time.

25
00:01:11.567 --> 00:01:13.733
Here's a simplified version in Python.

26
00:01:14.033 --> 00:01:16.400
Two splitters: big parents, small children.

27
00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:20.700
We embed only the small children, and tag each with its parent's id.

28
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.400
At query time we match children, then return their parents.

29
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:27.000
Here's a test you can run.

30
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:32.967
Chunk one sixty page propulsion report three ways: fixed, recursive, and parent-child.

31
00:01:33.267 --> 00:01:38.600
Fixed size splits a critical valve failure table mid-row, and misses two of five questions.

32
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:43.433
Parent-child gets all five, because the child hit pulls in the whole procedure.

33
00:01:44.633 --> 00:01:45.379
Let's recap.

34
00:01:45.379 --> 00:01:47.867
Always split documents before embedding.

35
00:01:48.167 --> 00:01:51.100
Strategies climb from fixed size to parent-child.

36
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.967
Small chunks retrieve, big chunks inform.

37
00:01:54.267 --> 00:01:57.011
And compare chunkers on your own questions.

38
00:01:57.011 --> 00:01:59.500
Next up: vector databases, the concept.
